I played this game for about an hour. It has some issues I just was not comfortable with:
- Weapons feel weird / underpowered and sometimes do not seem to hit as intented. For instance try destroying the glass with the chainsaw, it will work at certain distance, but when you move closer to the glass and continue cutting, the weapon sort of clips through and does not cause any damage;
- Sometimes weird physics, jumps and falling objects just did not seem right;
- Enemies clipping through walls, noticeably the spider-like enemies;
- Stupid AI - enemies often stand at one place and just fire, even when they are behind a wall;
- Invisible walls - there are points that look like they should be accessible, and than an invisible wall stops you. It is kind of cheap to make level design like this;
- Boredom after some time.

// Don't buy the game in its current state. It is on a good track, but it's far from good.
There is very little content and no challenge whatsoever. It is impossible to lose the game or make it difficult in anyway. Very soon after starting a new game, you will realise that you have already build all the necessary buildings and that good placement of buildings is not very important. Any further time spend playing is incredibly repetative.
That said, the game does remind me a bit of caesar II and is certainly better than Banishment.

This game is different than pretty much every other game I've played before. Different is good. With this game it's great actually. The only quality hardcore rpg really worth playing that I've come across is the dark souls series...Until this game. It does not hold your hand. You have got to figure things out yourself and combat is hard to master but holy♥♥♥♥♥♥it feels satisfying when you get good at it. Getting a really solid hit on a difficult enemy in the face with a sword that knocks them to the ground feels sooooo satisfying that I seriously feel like a true warrior. When I first played the game I definitely got frustrated at combat multiple times and almost gave up but I am so glad I didn't because it is a really well thought out combat system. I mean this is truly innovative stuff. Physics based anything in videogames is usually used in a humoruous light because it's really hard to get right without coming off as a joke but these guys did it for real. Every once in a while something humorous happens like tripping over a chair or a plank in the middle of the batle but most of the time these fights are intense dynamic battles with enemies that fight like real people (or monsters). The AI are really good on the enemies. Also the dungeon is actually more like a real place where loot is realistic placed. Many rooms are just what they are. Rooms with some cups and furniture and maybe some clothes. Nothing special. A bathroom is just that. It's a bathroom. This game is all about realism but it is really well done and worth your time. I seriously reccomend giving this game a try, and I mean a good try that isn't just an hour. Spend some time to get familiar with combat in arena mode and maybe watch some youtube videos if you are having problems. Also in campaign mode you don't have to kill every creature you see. Some are (semi) neutral and it took me a little while to realize that.
tl;dr;
If you are into hardcore rpg games or want to get into them I really really reccomend this game. Give it a few hours before making a judgement on this game. Combat can be a little tough to get into but when you do it is worth it 100x over.

Pros:
-great atmosphere reminiscent of intelligent (mostly Russian) SF (Solaris, Stalker, Aelita...);
-nice, slightly arty, mostly linear story;
-being relatively short (4-6 hrs.) it does not overstay its welcome.
Cons:
-jetpack platforming is only semi-decent due to camera issues and the inability to reverse course in mid air;
-the puzzles involving the robotic arm are very easy and repetitive;
-mineral collectibles are pointless.
Conclusion:
-For the fans of thinking person's SF more interested in the story than super tight game mechanics and flashy graphics, not averse to more artistic gaming experiences in the vein of 'Papo & Yo', Contrast' or 'Dear Esther' (but with actual gameplay and fail states) - an enjoyable ride.

Creeper World is a unique take on the traditional tower defense game.  Instead of fending off waves of path-following zombies, you must protect your command center from the countinuously flowing of Creeper.
If you enjoy defense games, this is definitely one to try out, and there are a few other versions of this game available for free online if you're not sure you want to invest the money.
//==========
//   Good
//==========
+ Unique take on tower defense games
+ Story mode + replayable levels
//==========
//   Bad
//==========
- Mediocre graphics
- Story is somewhat difficult to understand
- Free online versions with similar content

Regalia: Of Men and Monarchs is one of the best Turn-Based RPG's I've ever played. Well written characters, enjoyable (but quite hard) combat system, ability to expand the castle and fortify the bonds with other people, and not to mention that this game is full of jokes and references. My only complain is that we can't marry Aliss :P ... Well that and the fact that game ended ;v;
I reccomend it to fans that loves turn-based system games, anime (because it's in anime style) as well as comedy.
I hope to play the second one if it'll ever come out.

